---
/**
 * foodcraft / ServicesBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar": THE MENU.
 *
 * ROLE. The design's SERVICES element (id="menu"), which is the page's drinks
 * list: a CENTRED head — lilac eyebrow pill, display h2 with its purple
 * emphasis, one narrow note — over a THREE-CARD GRID of white 26px-radius
 * cards. Each card opens with a 52px colour disc and a small tracked-caps
 * badge on the same line, then the drink name, its blurb, and a foot pinned to
 * the bottom by `margin-top:auto` carrying a 12px size line left and the
 * display-face price hard right. Under the grid sits the design's PINK
 * add-on band: a title and a note on the left, cream price pills on the right.
 * There is no rule, no border and no hairline anywhere in this section — the
 * card is a filled plate, exactly as the design draws it.
 *
 * THE THREE HUES, since this is the section that touches all of them.
 *   · The card ground is --card and its type is --ink / --ink-soft / --muted.
 *   · --accent (purple) appears ONLY on --paper and --card grounds: the price,
 *     the add-on pill's price, and the headline emphasis the wrapper paints.
 *     --accent-deep is what sits on --lilac (the eyebrow, the card badge).
 *   · --pink is a GROUND and never text. The add-on band takes the wrapper's
 *     .band-pink rather than setting a background and then picking a colour,
 *     and every string on it is --on-pink or --plum. Purple on pink is 3.93
 *     (computed below) and is the one pairing this palette cannot have.
 *   · --sage appears once here and only as a small decorative disc, which is
 *     how the design itself uses it — never as this section's band.
 * Nothing here is prefixed with a sibling's class names: every scoped class is
 * `.sip-menu-*`, matching AboutBO's `.sip-about-*` for this template. An audit
 * had to rename a whole set copied verbatim between two files in this family.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every number is lifted from "Sipsip Tea Bar
 * Landing.dc.html" and none is re-derived: the centred head, the 16px grid
 * gutter, the 22px card padding, the 12px card stack gap, the 52px disc, the
 * 10px/.1em badge, the 24px/1.18 drink name, the 14px/1.7 blurb, the 14px foot
 * offset, the 12px/600 size line, the 23px price, the band's 16px offset,
 * 26px/30px padding, 25px title, 14.5px/1.65 note and 10px/16px pills. Hex maps
 * to the token holding the SAME value (R1), so no literal hex appears below:
 *   #FFFFFF --card · #241A22 --ink · #5D5158 --ink-soft · #6B6067 --muted
 *   #7B4FA8 --accent · #5C3580 --accent-deep · #EEE2F5 --lilac
 *   #F2C6DA --pink · #3D1B2C --on-pink · #5C2A45 --plum · #FFF7F2 --paper
 *   #E3EFDC --sage · 26px --r-lg · 100px --r-pill.
 * ONE COLOUR HAS NO TOKEN and is reported rather than invented: the card
 * badge's ground is #F6F1F8 in the design, and no PageBO token holds that
 * value. --lilac (#EEE2F5) is the token in the same family and is what the
 * design uses for every other label ground on the page, so the badge takes it.
 * That is a deliberate half-step darker than the design, not an oversight.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every path below is DECLARED in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts's `services` group, so none is a dead hook:
 *   services.tag                    — the lilac eyebrow pill (.kick)
 *   services.headline               — h2, set:html so the owner's own <em>
 *                                     lands where THEY put it in THEIR sentence
 *   services.sub                    — the centred note under the headline
 *   services.items.${i}.image       — data-image-field, the 52px disc
 *   services.items.${i}.tag         — the card's tracked-caps badge
 *   services.items.${i}.title       — the drink name (h3)
 *   services.items.${i}.desc        — the blurb
 *   services.items.${i}.note        — the foot's small size line, left
 *   services.items.${i}.price       — the display-face price, right
 *   services.whole.headline         — the add-on band's title
 *   services.whole.body             — the add-on band's note
 *   services.whole.tag              — the band pill's label
 *   services.whole.price            — the band pill's purple price
 *
 * DERIVED — three things, all decorative, none of them copy:
 *   · the disc's colour when there is no upload. The design gives each drink a
 *     literal swatch hex of its own (#C89B6A, #A886C4, #7A9B5C …) and R1
 *     forbids writing one, so an unfilled disc cycles --pink / --lilac / --sage
 *     by DISPLAY position (R6 — it is decoration, so it must not jump when a
 *     blank row above it is dropped). The disc is always drawn, as the design
 *     always draws it, and it is aria-hidden until it carries a real photo.
 *   · the reveal delay, likewise by display position.
 *   · the card FILTER and the section gate. Nothing else on this section is
 *     computed: every mark is either a string the owner typed or a shape CSS
 *     draws.
 *
 * NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE (R2). The design's six demo drinks are the
 * DESIGNER filling their own template; a drink nobody sells is an invented
 * drink and ₱110 nobody quoted is an invented price, so not one of them is a
 * fallback. The price is printed VERBATIM — never converted, never given a
 * currency symbol this file chose, never totalled or compared across cards.
 * Every guard sits OUTSIDE its element, so an empty value removes the ELEMENT:
 * no empty badge, no heading over nothing, no foot under a card that carries
 * neither a size line nor a price, and no add-on band when every one of its
 * four fields is blank.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, DELIBERATELY:
 *   · THE CATEGORY PILL STRIP, and this is the one omission a reader will
 *     otherwise take for carelessness. The design draws a centred row of
 *     category pills between the head and the grid, fed by a `categories` list
 *     — a list genericContentSchema.ts does not declare anywhere. Every
 *     available alternative is worse than leaving it out: writing the labels
 *     here would be this component supplying copy (R2), and re-using
 *     services.items[].tag would print every card's badge a second time, above
 *     the cards it already sits on — the design's two lists are different data
 *     (its demo strip reads "Milk tea / Fruit tea / Matcha", its badges read
 *     "Bestseller / Mild / Dairy free"). So the strip is dropped — and the head
 *     margin below does NOT try to reproduce the design's head-to-grid distance,
 *     because that distance IS the strip: the head's 14px bottom margin collapses
 *     with the strip's 26px top margin (adjacent in-flow siblings, so 26px, not
 *     40px), then the strip's own pill height, then its 30px bottom margin. None
 *     of that survives the strip's removal. What is used instead is the design's
 *     OWN margin for a centred head sitting DIRECTLY over a grid — 30px, the
 *     value its GALLERY, TESTIMONIALS, LOCATION and FAQ heads each set. If a
 *     `services.categories` list is ever declared, the strip is a wrapping
 *     centred flex row, gap 9px, of 13px/700 pills at 11px/20px padding with
 *     the first pill filled --accent/--on-accent and the rest --card/--ink-soft,
 *     and the head margin goes back to the design's 14px.
 *   · THE ADD-ON PILLS AS A LIST. The design's band draws a wrapping ROW of five
 *     pills from an `addOns` list. genericContentSchema.ts declares no such list
 *     — only the single whole-package pair services.whole.tag + .price — so
 *     exactly ONE pill is drawn, from the pair the schema does own. Writing a
 *     second and third pill would be this file inventing add-ons nobody sells.
 *     The row keeps the design's flex/wrap/8px-gap shape, so a declared list
 *     would drop into it without changing anything else here.
 *   · services.whole.unit, the band's price unit. The design's pill is a label
 *     and a price and nothing else; appending a unit it does not draw would be
 *     this file inventing layout. (ServicesBM drops services.items[].duration
 *     from its foot for the same reason.)
 *   · services.items[].meta, .features. The design's card is disc / badge /
 *     name / blurb / size line / price and nothing else, and a data-field for
 *     something the section never draws is a sidebar input that silently
 *     changes nothing.
 *   · NO RATING AND NO STAR. The design's menu card prints neither, so this
 *     section makes no rating claim at all. services.items[].rating is read by
 *     the templates that draw one; here it would be a hook on nothing. (Family
 *     rule, restated because it is the easiest one to break: N stars are drawn
 *     only from a numeric rating field, clamped 1–5, aria-hidden with the
 *     number itself in the accessible label — never five stars over an entry
 *     that carried none.)
 *   · services.ctaLabel / services.ctaHref, and therefore NO onPage() HELPER
 *     (R7). The design's menu carries no link on the card and no action on the
 *     section — this page's actions live in the header, the hero and the
 *     closing band — so this file emits no href, declares no data-href-field,
 *     and a remap helper with nothing to remap would be dead code. Inventing a
 *     button to give it a job is exactly the trade this family forbids. The
 *     anchor duty this file DOES own is its id="menu", one of PageBO's six page
 *     anchors and the target the header nav points at. If a link is ever added
 *     here, copy onPage() from a BO sibling with
 *       PAGE_ANCHORS = {menu, about, gallery, visit, faq, main}
 *       ANCHOR_ALIAS = {location→visit, contact→visit, services→menu,
 *                       rooms→menu, host→about, why→about, work→gallery,
 *                       book→main}
 *     and pass tel:/mailto:/http: through untouched.
 *   · any button class at all, so none of .btn--fill / .btn--pink / .btn--cream
 *     / .btn--line appears below, and neither does any engine-pinned name
 *     (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust .testi, or `.hero` on
 *     a <section>) — R4.
 *   · the wrapper's .chip primitive. It is a 12px purple-on-lilac label at
 *     7px/15px; this section needs a 10px uppercase badge and a 13px cream
 *     pill, so both are written out rather than fought with five overrides.
 *   · services.headline is the only set:html field here. The drink name and
 *     every pill are plain text, as the design prints them — and a set:html
 *     child could not be reached by this scoped block anyway (R3).
 *
 * THE CARD FILTER. A card counts as real when it carries a photo, a title, a
 * desc, a badge, a note OR a price — the six things this card can draw. All six
 * are counted on purpose: each is something an owner put into a live input, so
 * dropping a card that holds only a photo, or only a price, would silently
 * delete their work. A row holding none of them is an unfilled "+ Add" row and
 * would spend a whole white plate on nothing visible.
 *
 * INDEXES (R6). Blank rows are dropped, but every data-field and
 * data-image-field carries the row's ORIGINAL array index, so deleting card 2
 * cannot misdirect an edit into card 3. The two things that are DECORATIVE —
 * the disc's colour and the reveal delay — use the DISPLAY position instead,
 * which is what keeps the colour rhythm even after a blank row is dropped.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills services.tag,
 * services.headline AND services.sub on every site (it derives the sub from the
 * business name) while leaving services.items at [] — so a gate that counted
 * the tag or the headline would ship a centred heading and a note over an empty
 * grid on every site that listed nothing. The gate counts BODY content only: at
 * least one real card, or a filled add-on band. services.whole.* is NOT derived
 * anywhere, so it is safe to count.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY (R9). The disc is announced only when it holds a real upload
 * AND the owner gave that drink a name: then it is role="img" labelled by THAT
 * NAME — the owner's own words — and in every other case it is hidden from the
 * tree rather than announced as "image", because inventing "Drink photo" would
 * publish a description of something nobody described. The three attributes are
 * mutually exclusive and ALL conditional, so no literal aria-hidden ever sits
 * beside a conditional one — the duplicated-attribute bug where the literal
 * wins and hides content that should have been announced. The <h2> is what
 * announces this section; nothing decorative here carries a name.
 *
 * CONTRAST — COMPUTED from PageBO's own token values (sRGB relative luminance,
 * WCAG 2.x), not estimated, and only for pairs this section actually paints:
 *   --accent-deep on --lilac  7.40  (eyebrow pill, card badge)
 *   --ink on --paper         15.92  (h2)   · --accent on --paper  5.63 (h2 em)
 *   --ink-soft on --paper     7.13  (the centred note)
 *   --ink on --card          16.86  (drink name)
 *   --ink-soft on --card      7.54  (blurb)
 *   --muted on --card         6.01  (the 12px size line)
 *   --accent on --card        5.96  (the price)
 *   --on-pink on --pink       9.96  (band title)
 *   --plum on --pink          7.41  (band note)
 *   --on-pink on --paper     14.26  (band pill label)
 *   --accent on --paper       5.63  (band pill price — the pill's ground is
 *                                    CREAM, not the pink it sits on)
 * Nothing on this section is below AA, including the 10px badge and the 12px
 * size line. For the record and as the reason the band pill is cream: --accent
 * on --pink is 3.93, under the 4.5 floor, which is why no purple is ever set
 * directly on the pink ground here.
 *   ONE SCHEME CAVEAT: PageBO records that --muted is rewritten to
 *   palette.inkSoft by genericThemeOverrides.ts, so under a scheme pick the
 *   12px size line and the blurb converge on one colour. That is not something
 *   to re-derive around: the design draws the size line at #6B6067 and --muted
 *   is the token that holds it, which is what R1 asks for.
 *
 * RESPONSIVE (R8). The wrapper owns the shared 760/520 pass and the whole
 * vertical rhythm (`section { padding: clamp(48px,6.4vw,80px) 0 }`); that is
 * page-level and is deliberately not restated for one section. This file owns
 * its GRID: three tracks to two at 900 (below that a 24px drink name inside
 * 22px of padding has less measure at three-across than the design ever gave
 * it), and two to one at 620. Every track is minmax(0,1fr) and every
 * owner-typed string breaks anywhere, so at 360px nothing can push the page
 * sideways — a long unbroken price, a one-word badge typed in caps and a
 * hyphen-free add-on label are the likeliest strings here to arrive
 * unbreakable, and all three are capped at 100% of their pill and broken.
 * CLAMPS ARE MONOTONIC ACROSS BREAKPOINTS: every size this file states is a
 * FLAT value, and the only three TYPE sizes that change at a breakpoint decrease
 * (24px→22px name, 23px→21px price, 25px→22px band title), so nothing on this
 * section can grow as the viewport narrows. Every non-type value that moves
 * decreases too — head margin 30→24, card padding 22→20, disc 52→46, band
 * padding 26/30→22/20, band gap 24→18. The 760 pass releases two measure caps
 * (46ch and 52ch) and releasing a cap changes no type size.
 *   THE CLAMP IT INHERITS IS NOT MONOTONIC, and a reader tracing R8 through
 *   this file deserves to know that is a WRAPPER fault rather than a clean
 *   pass: PageBO's .h2 is clamp(28px,3.9vw,52px) and becomes clamp(26px,7vw,
 *   38px) at max-width:760px, so the rendered headline STEPS UP from 29.7px to
 *   38px as the viewport crosses 760 (3.9vw is already near its 28px floor
 *   there, while 7vw is pinned to its 38px cap until 543px). It is deliberately
 *   not patched from here: restating a size on .sip-menu-h would fix one of the
 *   fifteen sections that render an .h2, leave AboutBO — which takes the shared
 *   curve — a different size at the same viewport, and hide the fault from the
 *   other fourteen. It is reported against the wrapper. The design's own
 *   headline clamp for this section, clamp(30px,4vw,52px), differs from the
 *   wrapper's by two pixels of floor, so taking the shared curve costs the
 *   design nothing. (The design's 1.08 leading against the wrapper's 1.12 is
 *   left with the wrapper for the same reason, and AboutBO leaves it there
 *   too — forking it here would put one of fifteen headlines out of step with
 *   the other fourteen.)
 *
 * SPECIFICITY (R3). PageBO wraps its primitives in :where(), so an Astro-scoped
 * rule here at (0,2,0) beats the global at (0,1,1) — media queries included. So
 * never restate a property the wrapper tunes per breakpoint without restating
 * it at that breakpoint too (that is why the note's measure is released again
 * below), and never restate a heading's face or letter-spacing: the wrapper's
 * heading rule owns them. WEIGHT is the one exception and the design forces it —
 * its card h3 is 600 while its band h3 is 700, and PageBO pins every heading to
 * 700, so .sip-menu-name restates 600 and .sip-menu-band-h leaves the wrapper's
 * 700 alone. The band's h3 is the one heading whose
 * COLOUR is restated, and it has to be: `:where(.band-pink)` only sets an
 * inherited colour, while the wrapper's `:where(h1,h2,h3,h4){color:var(--ink)}`
 * sets it directly on the element and would win. The purple emphasis inside the
 * headline arrives through set:html AFTER scoping, so it is unreachable from
 * this block by design — the wrapper's global `.h2 em` rule paints it, and
 * nothing here needs :global() precisely because of that.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const services = (content && content.services) || {};
const str = (v: any) => (typeof v === 'string' ? v.trim() : '');

// `services` is a wrapper OR a bare legacy array, so every scalar read has to
// tolerate both — on an array these are simply undefined, which is what the
// guards below expect.
const tag = str(services.tag);
const headline = str(services.headline);
const sub = str(services.sub);

type Item = { title?: string; desc?: string; tag?: string; note?: string; price?: string; image?: string };
// Both shapes: the wrapped `{tag, headline, sub, items[]}` and the legacy bare
// array at content.services (see lib/services-shape.ts).
const rawItems: any[] = Array.isArray(services.items)
  ? services.items
  : (Array.isArray(content?.services) ? content.services : []);

// …and all three VOCABULARIES. lib/astro-builder.ts maps name→title /
// description→desc when content.services arrives as a bare array, but NOT when
// it arrives already wrapped — and a wrapped row still carrying the legacy key
// names is exactly what the editor's brief draft-normalisation window wrote.
// Without this mapping every row on such a site fails the "did the owner fill
// anything in?" filter below and the WHOLE MENU vanishes while the data sits
// untouched in the record. A bare string row (the oldest shape of all) becomes
// its own title rather than being spread into character keys and lost.
const items: Item[] = rawItems.map((it: any) => {
  if (typeof it === 'string') return { title: it.trim() };
  if (!it || typeof it !== 'object') return {};
  return {
    title: str(it.title) || str(it.name),
    desc: str(it.desc) || str(it.description),
    tag: str(it.tag),
    note: str(it.note),
    price: str(it.price),
    image: str(it.image),
  };
});

// Drop the rows an owner added and never filled — carrying the ORIGINAL index
// (R6). Only the six fields this card DRAWS count; see THE CARD FILTER above.
const cards = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ ...it, i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.image || r.title || r.desc || r.tag || r.note || r.price));

// The add-on band. `whole` is absent on every legacy record and on a bare
// array, so it is normalised to {} before any read.
const whole = (services && typeof services.whole === 'object' && services.whole) || {};
const bandTitle = str((whole as any).headline);
const bandNote = str((whole as any).body);
const bandLabel = str((whole as any).tag);
const bandPrice = str((whole as any).price);
const hasBandCopy = Boolean(bandTitle || bandNote);
const hasBandPill = Boolean(bandLabel || bandPrice);
const hasBand = hasBandCopy || hasBandPill;

// An apostrophe in an upload's filename would otherwise close the url('') early
// and take the whole background-image declaration down with it. The RAW value
// still travels into data-image-field, which is what the editor reads back.
const cssUrl = (s: string) => s.replace(/'/g, '%27');

// R5: body content only. tag / headline / sub are all derived on every site.
const hasSection = cards.length > 0 || hasBand;
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="sip-menu" id="menu">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline || sub) && (
        <div class="sip-menu-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick sip-menu-kick" data-field="services.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 sip-menu-h" data-field="services.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
          {sub && <p class="sip-menu-note" data-field="services.sub">{sub}</p>}
        </div>
      )}

      {cards.length > 0 && (
        <div class="sip-menu-grid">
          {cards.map((it, k: number) => (
            <article class="sip-menu-card reveal" data-delay={String((k % 3) + 1)}>
              {/* The disc is ALWAYS drawn — it is the design's own mark and it
                  is CSS, not copy — and it is always a live image slot, so an
                  owner can fill it from the page as well as the sidebar. With
                  no upload it keeps its cycled token ground; with one it takes
                  the photo, and the ground stays underneath while that loads.
                  All three a11y attributes are conditional and mutually
                  exclusive (R9). */}
              <div class="sip-menu-top">
                <span
                  class={`sip-menu-disc sip-menu-disc--${(k % 3) + 1}`}
                  data-image-field={`services.items.${it.i}.image`}
                  role={it.image && it.title ? 'img' : undefined}
                  aria-label={it.image && it.title ? it.title : undefined}
                  aria-hidden={it.image && it.title ? undefined : 'true'}
                  style={it.image ? `background-image:url('${cssUrl(it.image)}');` : undefined}
                ></span>
                {it.tag && (
                  <span class="mono sip-menu-badge" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.tag`}>{it.tag}</span>
                )}
              </div>
              {it.title && <h3 class="sip-menu-name" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.title`}>{it.title}</h3>}
              {it.desc && <p class="sip-menu-copy" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.desc`}>{it.desc}</p>}
              {/* Guarded OUTSIDE the foot, so a card carrying neither a size
                  line nor a price draws no foot offset under nothing. */}
              {(it.note || it.price) && (
                <div class="sip-menu-foot">
                  {it.note && <span class="sip-menu-size" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.note`}>{it.note}</span>}
                  {/* Printed exactly as typed. `margin-left:auto` keeps it on
                      the design's right edge even when the size line beside it
                      is absent and space-between has nothing to push against. */}
                  {it.price && <span class="fig sip-menu-price" data-field={`services.items.${it.i}.price`}>{it.price}</span>}
                </div>
              )}
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      )}

      {hasBand && (
        /* .band-pink is the wrapper's own pairing: it brings the pink ground
           AND the deep-plum text colour together, so this file never sets a
           background and then picks a colour for it. */
        <div class="sip-menu-band band-pink reveal">
          {hasBandCopy && (
            <div class="sip-menu-band-copy">
              {bandTitle && <h3 class="sip-menu-band-h" data-field="services.whole.headline">{bandTitle}</h3>}
              {bandNote && <p class="sip-menu-band-p" data-field="services.whole.body">{bandNote}</p>}
            </div>
          )}
          {hasBandPill && (
            <div class="sip-menu-band-pills">
              {/* The design's pill is a label and a purple price side by side,
                  separated by a literal space in its markup; an inline-flex gap
                  puts the same space in the same place while letting each half
                  be guarded on its own — a price with no label prints no
                  leading space, and a label with no price no trailing one. */}
              <span class="sip-menu-pill">
                {bandLabel && <span data-field="services.whole.tag">{bandLabel}</span>}
                {bandPrice && <span class="sip-menu-pill-price" data-field="services.whole.price">{bandPrice}</span>}
              </span>
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* One scoped block. Read the SPECIFICITY note in the frontmatter before adding
     to it. (Never write a literal end-of-stylesheet tag inside a comment here,
     not even as an example — this is a raw-text element and the parser would end
     the stylesheet on the spot, silently truncating the rest of the file.)

     `.sip-menu` itself carries NO rule, on purpose and not by omission: PageBO's
     shared `section { padding: clamp(48px,6.4vw,80px) 0 }` is the whole of this
     section's outer rhythm, and the class is here only so the section is
     nameable from a devtools inspection. */

  /* ── THE CENTRED HEAD. The design's own 14px bottom margin here exists only to
        sit above the category strip, and it COLLAPSES with that strip's 26px top
        margin anyway; with the strip not rendered (see NOT RENDERED) none of that
        spacing survives. 30px is the margin the design gives a centred head that
        sits DIRECTLY over a grid — GALLERY, TESTIMONIALS, LOCATION and FAQ each
        set exactly that. */
  .sip-menu-head { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 30px; }

  /* The eyebrow is the wrapper's .kick — its 11px, its 700 and its uppercase
     stay there, and the shared 520 pass still shrinks it to 10.5px. What the
     design adds for THIS template is the lilac pill: a ground, a pill radius,
     8px/16px of padding, the slightly tighter .14em tracking and --accent-deep
     rather than --accent, because accent-deep on lilac is 7.40 where accent on
     lilac is 4.77. .kick is inline-block already, so text-align centres it. */
  .sip-menu-kick {
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    background-color: var(--lilac);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Margins and measure only. Size, face, colour, the purple <em> and both of
     the wrapper's mobile passes stay with .h2 — see the R8 note about the shared
